{"decisions": [
{"item_type": "decision", "point": "D4", "problem": "Whether to continue joint research work on scheming with OpenAI after the current project.", "did": "Chose to continue collaborating on future projects covering science of scheming, anti-scheming, and monitoring.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "Scheming is a problem for all providers and humanity, not just one lab.", "quote": "And we also want to continue working with them, and will, on future projects related to science of scheming, anti-scheming, monitoring, all of these kinds of things.", "off_topic": false},
{"item_type": "advice", "point": "D2", "problem": "How AI companies should handle access and permissions given to internally deployed AI systems.", "did": "Recommends privileged access where the AI does not get access by default and only when necessary, plus mapping angles of attack and deploying mitigations.", "alternatives_named": ["give the AI system access to literally everything"], "stated_reason": "Giving access to everything is particularly risky.", "quote": "If you give the AI system access to literally everything, that seems particularly risky. What you should be doing instead is maybe more privileged access, where you don't give it access by default, and you only give it access when particularly necessary.", "off_topic": false},
{"item_type": "advice", "point": "D1", "problem": "What categories frontier safety policies should cover when assessing AI risk.", "did": "Recommends treating internal deployment as a key category in frontier safety policies, especially for AI R&D and scheming.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "Risks from AI R&D and scheming are initially largely concentrated in internal rather than external deployment.", "quote": "in the frontier safety policies, consider internal deployment as a key category. Or at least among the different categories, consider how internal deployment could affect it. Especially for AI R&D and scheming, I think this would be a big upgrade", "off_topic": false},
{"item_type": "advice", "point": "D2", "problem": "Whether companies should evaluate internally deployed models and share results.", "did": "Agrees companies should evaluate internal models' capabilities and propensities, and governments should potentially be informed.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "Internal deployment evaluations are implied by frontier safety policies but should be made explicit.", "quote": "I think evaluations for these internally deployed models is definitely part of the thing that would have been implied by putting that into your frontier safety policies. But it's good to make it explicit that the companies should know what these models are capable of and what their propensities are", "off_topic": false},
{"item_type": "disagreement", "point": "D5", "problem": "How to interpret and model the most likely path to AI catastrophe.", "did": "Argues previous single-mechanism catastrophe narratives are too clean and isolated; instead expects multiple failure modes to interact and explode simultaneously.", "alternatives_named": ["one AI, a single AI, is misaligned, and then it takes over the world", "one actor that misuses the AI and that leads to a new pandemic"], "stated_reason": "All of the scenarios are plausible and have strong interaction effects.", "quote": "These other narratives are often very isolated, very clean and specific causal mechanisms — where one AI, a single AI, is misaligned, and then it takes over the world and then you have a catastrophe.", "off_topic": false},
{"item_type": "disagreement", "point": "D5", "problem": "Which threat model of AI takeover deserves concern — narrow self-exfiltration scenarios or broader scenarios.", "did": "Argues the risk is broader than self-exfiltration: the model could take over its own company or embed itself in government.", "alternatives_named": ["self-exfiltration"], "stated_reason": "Multiple large-scale societal risk pathways exist beyond the commonly discussed one.", "quote": "It doesn't necessarily just have to be self-exfiltration though. This is also one of the worries: it could also be that the model stays in the AI company and tries to take over that AI company over time, or influence leadership and influence decisions.", "off_topic": false},
{"item_type": "advice", "point": "D3", "problem": "What kind of accountability mechanisms to rely on rather than strong hard restrictions on AI companies.", "did": "Recommends softer measures like third-party interaction, external accountability and audits to force companies to explain and double-check their choices.", "alternatives_named": ["strong restrictions"], "stated_reason": "Strong restrictions are undesirable, but relying on good faith alone can be adversarially abused.", "quote": "I think softer things, like you should be interacting with third parties, you should get some external accountability: there should be at least some incentive for you to even explain the choices that you have made.", "off_topic": false},
{"item_type": "rejected", "point": "D4", "problem": "Whether the dangers of internal deployment can be ignored given the costs of guardrails.", "did": "Warns against the status quo intuition that internally deployed systems need no guardrails just because models were not dangerous in the past.", "alternatives_named": ["in the past, models just haven't been dangerous", "just by sheer status quo intuition"], "stated_reason": "The mindset that internal systems are safe is a relic of an earlier era and no longer applies.", "quote": "in the past, models just haven't been dangerous. Everything you did, there was almost no chance that this gets meaningfully misaligned or anything like this. So in the past, there's just no reason for you to assume that any of this is a problem.", "off_topic": false}
]}
